1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:06,556
Every force problem in IB Physics comes
down to two moves: draw the forces,

2
00:00:06,596 --> 00:00:08,728
then split them into components.

3
00:00:09,039 --> 00:00:12,450
So where does mg sin θ actually come from?

4
00:00:12,761 --> 00:00:14,224
Most people memorise it,

5
00:00:14,264 --> 00:00:17,770
and then put the sine and the
cosine on the wrong sides.

6
00:00:18,082 --> 00:00:21,589
We're going to build it instead,
so you never have to guess.

7
00:00:22,375 --> 00:00:26,850
In the next few minutes we'll
derive every contact force you need:

8
00:00:26,890 --> 00:00:31,199
the normal force, friction,
tension, buoyancy and viscous drag.

9
00:00:31,511 --> 00:00:35,099
No springs, no circles, no kinematics.

10
00:00:35,410 --> 00:00:40,041
Just forces, triangles, and the
trigonometry that connects them.

11
00:00:41,083 --> 00:00:44,093
First, the one tool
we'll use over and over.

12
00:00:44,404 --> 00:00:47,552
A force is an arrow with
a size and a direction,

13
00:00:47,592 --> 00:00:52,639
and any arrow can be replaced by two
perpendicular arrows that add up to it.

14
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:55,143
Splitting doesn't change the force.

15
00:00:55,455 --> 00:00:58,502
It just lets us deal with
one direction at a time.

16
00:00:59,298 --> 00:01:03,467
Make the force F the hypotenuse
of a right-angled triangle,

17
00:01:03,507 --> 00:01:05,576
at angle θ to the horizontal.

18
00:01:05,887 --> 00:01:11,949
Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, so
the side touching the angle is F cos θ.

19
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:18,026
Sine is opposite over hypotenuse, so the
side across from the angle is F sin θ.

20
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:23,883
Now swing the angle and watch: as one
component grows, the other shrinks,

21
00:01:23,923 --> 00:01:26,369
but together they always rebuild F.

22
00:01:27,149 --> 00:01:30,926
The rule to remember: cos
goes with the side touching θ,

23
00:01:30,966 --> 00:01:33,555
sin goes with the side across from it.

24
00:01:33,866 --> 00:01:35,427
Try it with numbers.

25
00:01:35,738 --> 00:01:38,535
A ten newton force at thirty degrees has

26
00:01:38,575 --> 00:01:41,868
eight point seven newtons
along the horizontal,

27
00:01:41,908 --> 00:01:44,177
and exactly five newtons across.

28
00:01:45,217 --> 00:01:48,307
Start simple: a block resting on a table.

29
00:01:48,619 --> 00:01:51,783
Its weight, mg, pulls straight down.

30
00:01:52,094 --> 00:01:55,018
The table pushes back up
with the normal force,

31
00:01:55,058 --> 00:01:57,833
normal meaning perpendicular
to the surface.

32
00:01:58,619 --> 00:02:02,271
Nothing accelerates, so the
vertical forces balance,

33
00:02:02,311 --> 00:02:04,513
and the normal force equals mg.

34
00:02:05,304 --> 00:02:08,115
But these two are not a
Newton's third law pair.

35
00:02:08,426 --> 00:02:09,994
Both act on the block.

36
00:02:10,305 --> 00:02:15,105
A third law pair is the same kind of
force acting on two different objects.

37
00:02:15,416 --> 00:02:18,913
The partner of the weight is the
block pulling the Earth upward.

38
00:02:19,698 --> 00:02:24,078
Push down on the block with your hand,
and the table pushes back harder.

39
00:02:24,389 --> 00:02:28,962
The normal force isn't fixed: it
responds to whatever it has to hold up.

40
00:02:29,274 --> 00:02:33,751
It only equals mg here because
nothing else is pushing up or down.

41
00:02:34,817 --> 00:02:38,869
Now pull the block with a rope
at angle θ above the horizontal.

42
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:46,104
Split the pull: F cos θ drags the block
sideways, and F sin θ lifts it a little.

43
00:02:46,891 --> 00:02:53,095
Vertically, everything still balances:
the normal force plus F sin θ equals mg.

44
00:02:53,406 --> 00:02:57,237
So the normal force is mg minus F sin θ.

45
00:02:57,549 --> 00:03:00,049
Pull harder and the
surface pushes back less,

46
00:03:00,089 --> 00:03:02,800
because the rope is
carrying part of the weight.

47
00:03:03,111 --> 00:03:07,811
Pull hard enough and the normal force
reaches zero: the block lifts off.

48
00:03:08,592 --> 00:03:14,196
Push down at the same angle instead, and
the vertical part adds to the weight:

49
00:03:14,236 --> 00:03:17,203
the normal force becomes mg plus F sin θ.

50
00:03:17,514 --> 00:03:22,440
Since friction is proportional to the
normal force, pulling up reduces friction,

51
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:24,343
and pushing down increases it.

52
00:03:25,383 --> 00:03:29,337
The normal force is also what you
feel through your feet in a lift.

53
00:03:29,648 --> 00:03:31,199
Take up as positive.

54
00:03:31,510 --> 00:03:35,820
Accelerating upward, the floor
must push harder than your weight.

55
00:03:36,132 --> 00:03:41,176
Newton's second law gives the
normal force minus mg equals ma,

56
00:03:41,216 --> 00:03:44,495
so the normal force is m times g plus a.

57
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:49,296
Accelerating downward,
it's m times g minus a.

58
00:03:49,607 --> 00:03:55,456
At constant speed there's no acceleration,
and the floor pushes with exactly mg.

59
00:03:55,767 --> 00:03:57,599
Your weight never changes.

60
00:03:57,910 --> 00:04:01,782
Only the contact force does,
and that's what the scales read.

61
00:04:02,093 --> 00:04:07,143
If the cable snapped, a would equal
g, and the normal force would be zero.

62
00:04:07,454 --> 00:04:09,894
That's what feeling
weightless really means.

63
00:04:10,950 --> 00:04:14,524
Now the classic: a block
on a slope at angle θ.

64
00:04:14,835 --> 00:04:19,276
Gravity doesn't care about the slope, so
the weight still points straight down.

65
00:04:19,587 --> 00:04:23,118
But the slope can only push
perpendicular to itself.

66
00:04:23,906 --> 00:04:28,374
So tilt your point of view to
match the slope: one axis along it,

67
00:04:28,414 --> 00:04:30,079
one perpendicular to it.

68
00:04:30,391 --> 00:04:34,234
That way the normal force
lands exactly on one axis.

69
00:04:34,545 --> 00:04:38,342
Now the weight is the only force
that isn't lined up with an axis,

70
00:04:38,382 --> 00:04:39,836
so it's the one we split.

71
00:04:40,625 --> 00:04:42,325
Here's the key geometry.

72
00:04:42,636 --> 00:04:46,628
Start with the slope flat: its
perpendicular points straight down,

73
00:04:46,668 --> 00:04:48,073
right along the weight.

74
00:04:48,384 --> 00:04:50,420
Now tilt the slope by θ.

75
00:04:50,731 --> 00:04:54,192
Its perpendicular tilts
by exactly the same θ.

76
00:04:54,503 --> 00:04:58,366
So the angle between the weight
and the perpendicular is θ.

77
00:04:59,155 --> 00:05:00,482
Read the triangle.

78
00:05:00,793 --> 00:05:06,204
The component touching θ, pressing
into the slope, is mg cos θ.

79
00:05:06,515 --> 00:05:11,776
The component across from θ,
pulling down the slope, is mg sin θ.

80
00:05:12,087 --> 00:05:17,231
Nothing moves into the slope, so
the normal force equals mg cos θ.

81
00:05:18,019 --> 00:05:19,470
A quick example.

82
00:05:19,781 --> 00:05:22,895
A two kilogram block on
a thirty degree slope:

83
00:05:22,935 --> 00:05:28,009
down the slope is nine point eight
newtons, and into the slope, seventeen.

84
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:30,312
Check the extremes.

85
00:05:30,623 --> 00:05:35,691
At zero degrees, sine is zero and
cosine is one: no pull along the slope,

86
00:05:35,731 --> 00:05:38,180
and the full weight on the surface.

87
00:05:38,491 --> 00:05:42,920
At ninety degrees the slope is a
wall: all of the weight acts along it,

88
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:44,534
and none presses into it.

89
00:05:44,845 --> 00:05:49,118
So if you ever forget which is
sine, tilt the slope almost flat,

90
00:05:49,158 --> 00:05:51,516
and ask which part should be small.

91
00:05:52,583 --> 00:05:53,635
Add friction.

92
00:05:53,946 --> 00:05:56,854
Push a heavy box gently
and it doesn't move,

93
00:05:56,894 --> 00:06:00,713
because friction pushes back
exactly as hard as you push.

94
00:06:01,024 --> 00:06:05,444
Push harder, and friction
rises to match, up to a limit:

95
00:06:05,484 --> 00:06:07,554
μs times the normal force.

96
00:06:07,865 --> 00:06:13,989
On the slope, static friction exactly
matches the down-slope pull, mg sin θ.

97
00:06:14,779 --> 00:06:16,021
Raise the angle.

98
00:06:16,332 --> 00:06:24,214
mg sin θ grows, while the maximum
friction, μs mg cos θ, shrinks.

99
00:06:24,525 --> 00:06:26,488
They meet at the tipping point.

100
00:06:27,273 --> 00:06:33,394
Set them equal: mg sin
θ equals μs mg cos θ.

101
00:06:33,705 --> 00:06:38,504
The mass cancels, divide by
cos θ, and μs equals tan θ,

102
00:06:38,544 --> 00:06:41,710
because sine over cosine is tangent.

103
00:06:42,021 --> 00:06:46,993
Measure the angle where a block just
starts to slip, and you've measured μs.

104
00:06:47,776 --> 00:06:51,804
Once it slides, friction drops
to μd times the normal force,

105
00:06:51,844 --> 00:06:55,572
because moving surfaces grip
less than stationary ones.

106
00:06:55,883 --> 00:07:02,899
So the resultant force down the
slope is mg sin θ minus μd mg cos θ.

107
00:07:03,950 --> 00:07:05,296
Here's a trickier one.

108
00:07:05,607 --> 00:07:09,482
Hold a block on a smooth slope
with a horizontal push F.

109
00:07:09,793 --> 00:07:14,129
Now F isn't lined up with either
axis, so it gets split too.

110
00:07:14,917 --> 00:07:18,031
The angle between F and
the slope is also θ,

111
00:07:18,071 --> 00:07:23,303
because F is horizontal and the slope
is tilted by θ from the horizontal.

112
00:07:23,614 --> 00:07:28,605
F is the hypotenuse, so F cos
θ pushes up along the slope,

113
00:07:28,645 --> 00:07:31,074
and F sin θ presses into it.

114
00:07:31,857 --> 00:07:38,511
Along the slope, F cos θ balances
mg sin θ, so F equals mg tan θ,

115
00:07:38,551 --> 00:07:42,258
because sine over cosine is tangent.

116
00:07:42,569 --> 00:07:47,920
Perpendicular to the slope, the
normal force must balance both:

117
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:49,843
mg cos θ plus F sin θ.

118
00:07:50,154 --> 00:07:54,514
The push adds to the normal force, so it
can even end up bigger than the weight.

119
00:07:55,550 --> 00:07:57,544
Tension is the pull in a string,

120
00:07:57,584 --> 00:08:00,825
directed along the string
and away from the object.

121
00:08:01,136 --> 00:08:04,099
A string can only pull, never push.

122
00:08:04,410 --> 00:08:08,703
Hang a mass from a single string
and the tension equals mg.

123
00:08:09,489 --> 00:08:14,271
Now hang a lamp from two strings,
each at angle α to the horizontal.

124
00:08:14,582 --> 00:08:20,353
Split each tension: T cos α
sideways, and T sin α upward.

125
00:08:20,664 --> 00:08:22,521
The sideways parts cancel.

126
00:08:22,833 --> 00:08:27,991
The upward parts share the
weight: two T sin α equals mg,

127
00:08:28,031 --> 00:08:30,767
so T equals mg over two sin α.

128
00:08:31,558 --> 00:08:36,479
Flatten the strings and watch: as
α shrinks, sin α heads toward zero,

129
00:08:36,519 --> 00:08:38,389
and the tension shoots up.

130
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:44,230
At thirty degrees each string carries
exactly mg, and at five degrees,

131
00:08:44,270 --> 00:08:45,862
nearly six times mg.

132
00:08:46,173 --> 00:08:49,685
That's why you can never pull a
washing line perfectly straight.

133
00:08:50,468 --> 00:08:56,343
With unequal angles, balance each
direction separately: sideways,

134
00:08:56,383 --> 00:09:00,620
T one cos α one equals
T two cos α two; upward,

135
00:09:00,660 --> 00:09:04,937
T one sin α one plus T
two sin α two equals mg.

136
00:09:05,248 --> 00:09:08,278
Two equations, two unknown tensions.

137
00:09:08,589 --> 00:09:12,446
Or slide the three forces
nose to tail: in equilibrium,

138
00:09:12,486 --> 00:09:14,399
they close into a triangle.

139
00:09:15,450 --> 00:09:21,414
Over a light, smooth pulley, tension
keeps its size and only changes direction.

140
00:09:21,726 --> 00:09:24,572
Tie a block on a table to a hanging mass.

141
00:09:24,883 --> 00:09:29,156
The same tension pulls the block
sideways and holds the hanging mass up.

142
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:33,529
Call the hanging mass little
m, and the block big M.

143
00:09:33,841 --> 00:09:38,252
For the hanging mass,
mg minus T equals ma.

144
00:09:38,564 --> 00:09:41,452
For the block, T equals M a.

145
00:09:41,763 --> 00:09:45,404
The string links them, so they
share the same acceleration.

146
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:52,718
Add the two equations and the tension
cancels: mg equals big M plus little m,

147
00:09:52,758 --> 00:09:53,558
times a.

148
00:09:53,750 --> 00:09:58,532
So the acceleration equals
mg over big M plus little m.

149
00:09:58,843 --> 00:10:02,225
Substitute that back into
the block's equation,

150
00:10:02,265 --> 00:10:07,217
and T equals big M times little m
times g, over big M plus little m.

151
00:10:07,528 --> 00:10:12,051
And while it accelerates, the tension
is always less than the hanging weight,

152
00:10:12,091 --> 00:10:14,698
because the block has
to be accelerated too.

153
00:10:15,750 --> 00:10:17,628
Now put a block in a fluid.

154
00:10:17,939 --> 00:10:19,921
Fluid pressure grows with depth,

155
00:10:19,961 --> 00:10:24,511
so the bottom face is pushed up harder
than the top face is pushed down.

156
00:10:24,822 --> 00:10:26,877
The pushes on the sides cancel.

157
00:10:27,188 --> 00:10:28,974
The difference is buoyancy.

158
00:10:29,766 --> 00:10:33,908
The extra pressure across the
block's height h is ρ g h.

159
00:10:34,219 --> 00:10:37,944
That's the weight of a column
of fluid, per unit area.

160
00:10:38,255 --> 00:10:43,933
It acts on the face area A, so
the buoyant force is ρ g h A,

161
00:10:43,973 --> 00:10:48,548
and since h times A is
the volume, that's ρ V g.

162
00:10:48,859 --> 00:10:52,189
Here ρ is the fluid's
density, not the block's,

163
00:10:52,229 --> 00:10:55,241
and V is the volume of fluid pushed aside.

164
00:10:55,552 --> 00:10:58,510
Picture that space filled
with fluid instead.

165
00:10:58,821 --> 00:11:02,571
It would just sit there, so the
fluid around it must be pushing up

166
00:11:02,611 --> 00:11:05,540
with exactly the weight of
the fluid that fills it.

167
00:11:06,320 --> 00:11:11,669
A floating object sinks until it
pushes aside its own weight of fluid:

168
00:11:11,709 --> 00:11:15,327
ρ times the submerged
volume times g equals mg.

169
00:11:16,383 --> 00:11:19,178
Move through a fluid, and it drags back.

170
00:11:19,489 --> 00:11:24,793
For a small sphere moving slowly
through smooth, layered flow,

171
00:11:24,833 --> 00:11:28,280
Stokes' law gives the drag: six π η r v.

172
00:11:28,591 --> 00:11:34,390
Here η is the viscosity, how thick and
sticky the fluid is, r is the radius,

173
00:11:34,430 --> 00:11:35,889
and v is the speed.

174
00:11:36,200 --> 00:11:37,698
It grows with speed.

175
00:11:38,485 --> 00:11:40,463
Drop a ball bearing into oil.

176
00:11:40,774 --> 00:11:44,322
Weight pulls down;
buoyancy and drag push up.

177
00:11:44,633 --> 00:11:46,759
As it speeds up, the drag grows,

178
00:11:46,799 --> 00:11:50,453
until the forces balance and
the speed stops changing.

179
00:11:50,764 --> 00:11:52,245
That's terminal speed.

180
00:11:53,030 --> 00:11:54,674
Write the balance:

181
00:11:54,714 --> 00:12:01,075
mg equals the buoyant force plus
six π η r times the terminal speed.

182
00:12:01,386 --> 00:12:06,802
Replace each mass with density times
four thirds π r cubed, and solve.

183
00:12:07,113 --> 00:12:12,963
Move the buoyant force across, cancel
π and one r, and divide by six η.

184
00:12:13,274 --> 00:12:16,419
Four thirds divided by six is two ninths.

185
00:12:16,730 --> 00:12:22,277
The terminal speed is two r squared
g, times the density difference,

186
00:12:22,317 --> 00:12:23,302
over nine η.

187
00:12:24,089 --> 00:12:25,629
Read it like a recipe.

188
00:12:25,941 --> 00:12:29,570
Double the radius and the
ball falls four times faster.

189
00:12:29,881 --> 00:12:33,673
Use oil twice as thick
and it falls half as fast.

190
00:12:34,717 --> 00:12:37,900
Every contact force
follows the same recipe.

191
00:12:38,211 --> 00:12:42,048
Isolate the object, and draw
only the forces acting on it.

192
00:12:42,359 --> 00:12:44,845
Choose axes that match the surface.

193
00:12:45,157 --> 00:12:50,121
Split each force with its triangle:
cos for the side touching the angle,

194
00:12:50,161 --> 00:12:51,829
sin for the side across.

195
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:54,010
Then balance each direction.

196
00:12:54,803 --> 00:12:56,395
Three slips to avoid.

197
00:12:56,706 --> 00:13:00,118
Never draw mass times
acceleration as a force.

198
00:13:00,429 --> 00:13:04,622
For buoyancy, use the fluid's
density, not the block's.

199
00:13:04,933 --> 00:13:08,646
And always ask which side of
the triangle touches the angle.

200
00:13:09,435 --> 00:13:13,072
So mg sin θ isn't a formula to memorise.

201
00:13:13,383 --> 00:13:15,587
It's just one side of a triangle.
